"""Hand-written English method descriptions, part 7: equipment Dumbbell.

Keyed by the v5 catalog slug (slugify of the canonical exercise name).
Merged by build_progression.py as the last description tier; a key that
matches no catalog row fails the build, so typos surface immediately.
"""

DESCRIPTIONS = {
    "bilateral_dumbbell_wrist_curl": "The bilateral dumbbell wrist curl curls both wrists together from a braced position, training the forearm flexors symmetrically. Keep the range full, from fingers hanging open to full flexion; light bells and high reps suit the wrists best.",
    "chest_supported_dumbbell_row": "The chest-supported dumbbell row rows two bells from an incline bench with the chest pinned to the pad, removing every trace of body English. Pull the elbows past the ribs and squeeze the shoulder blades; the bells hang fully at the bottom of each rep.",
    "chest_supported_dumbbell_shrug": "The chest-supported dumbbell shrug shrugs two heavy bells from a chest-down position on an incline bench, isolating the traps without the lower back in the equation. Keep the arms long and lift the shoulders toward the ears; a brief hold at the top does the work.",
    "double_dumbbell_kickstand_deadlift": "The double dumbbell kickstand deadlift hinges with two bells and one foot's toe kicked back behind the other, a hybrid between bilateral and single-leg work. The rear toe is a balance point only; load the front leg, push the hips back and keep both bells hugging the shins.",
    "double_dumbbell_overhead_carry": "The double dumbbell overhead carry walks with a bell locked out in each hand, pressing the shoulders and core to hold the ribcage down under two loads. Keep the biceps near the ears and the steps short; the carry ends when the lockout starts to drift.",
    "dumbbell_bench_pull": "The dumbbell bench pull lies chest-down on a high bench and rows a bell in each hand from below, pulling them to the underside of the bench. The pad pins the torso so the back does all the work; lower the bells under full control each rep.",
    "dumbbell_face_pull": "The dumbbell face pull hinges forward and pulls two bells high toward the face with the elbows wide, training the rear delts and the external rotators. Keep the torso still and finish with the elbows above the wrists; light weights and strict paths are the point.",
    "dumbbell_hip_thrust": "The dumbbell hip thrust drives the hips to full extension with a bell across the pelvis, a low-friction alternative to the barbell version. Squeeze the glutes at the top and keep the chin tucked; pad the bell on the hip bone for comfort.",
    "dumbbell_overhead_carry": "The dumbbell overhead carry walks with one bell locked out overhead, training shoulder stability and anti-side-bend core strength at once. Keep the bell stacked over the shoulder and the hip and the ribs down; switch sides at the halfway point.",
    "dumbbell_pistol_squat": "The dumbbell pistol squat holds a bell at the chest as counterweight while squatting to a single leg, making the balance point easier to find. Keep the standing heel down and the free leg long; the bell reaches forward as the hips sink back.",
    "dumbbell_push_press": "The dumbbell push press dips the knees slightly and drives two bells overhead with leg power, allowing heavier loads than the strict press. Time the dip and drive as one; the arms only finish the job the legs start.",
    "dumbbell_reverse_wrist_curl": "The dumbbell reverse wrist curl trains the wrist extensors, hinging the backs of the hands up from a braced forearm position. These muscles tire fast and respond to light, high-rep work; keep the forearms glued down and the movement in the wrists only.",
    "dumbbell_snatch": "The dumbbell snatch drives a single bell from the floor or the hang to overhead in one explosive pull, catching it locked out with a dip. It is a fast, forgiving full-body power lift; keep the bell close on the way up and punch through at the top.",
    "dumbbell_somersault_squat": "The dumbbell somersault squat holds a bell at the chest and squats while rolling the torso in a controlled forward circle, keeping constant tension on the quads through an honest range. Move slowly, so the circle feels like a controlled tilt rather than a wobble; the bell's front load keeps the spine tall.",
    "dumbbell_svend_press": "The dumbbell Svend press presses the top of a bell between both palms out from the chest, an isometric squeeze that lights up the inner pecs. Keep the squeeze relentless through the whole range; short range, high tension, high reps.",
    "dumbbell_tricep_extension": "The dumbbell tricep extension lowers a bell behind the head from an overhead grip, stretching the triceps' long head through its fullest range. Keep the elbows narrow and pointing forward; the deeper the stretch, the better the rep, within shoulder comfort.",
    "dumbbell_windmill": "The dumbbell windmill holds a bell overhead and hinges sideways to touch the floor with the free hand, keeping the eyes on the bell throughout. It stretches the hip and hamstring of one side while the shoulder stabilizes a load overhead; move slowly and keep the bell arm vertical.",
    "dumbbell_wrist_curl": "The dumbbell wrist curl curls the wrists up from a braced forearm position, training the flexors one or two hands at a time. Full range matters more than load; let the bell roll down to the fingertips, then curl it back with the fingers and wrist together.",
    "incline_hammer_curl": "The incline hammer curl curls from an incline bench with the arms hanging behind the torso, putting the biceps under a long stretch while the neutral grip targets the brachialis. Keep the elbows still and let the arm hang fully at the bottom; the stretch is the growth zone.",
    "jefferson_curl": "The Jefferson curl rolls the spine down one vertebra at a time while holding a light weight, then unrolls back to standing, loading the entire back line through flexion and extension. Start nearly unloaded and move with extreme control; it is a mobility-strength drill for the spine and hamstrings, not a heavy lift.",
    "one_arm_dumbbell_push_press": "The one-arm dumbbell push press drives a single bell overhead with a leg dip and punch, letting one shoulder handle heavier loads while the core resists the sideways lean. Keep the torso square to the front; the dip is small and the drive is violent.",
    "one_arm_dumbbell_swing": "The one-arm dumbbell swing swings a single bell between the legs to shoulder height with a powerful hip snap, the dumbbell cousin of the kettlebell swing. Keep the arm long and let the hips do the throwing; hike the bell back before every rep.",
    "preacher_hammer_curl": "The preacher hammer curl curls a bell with a neutral grip over a preacher pad, which fixes the elbow and puts the brachialis and forearm under strict tension. Let the arm straighten fully at the bottom of the pad's angle; the stretch there is the point of the exercise.",
    "seated_dumbbell_tricep_extension": "The seated dumbbell tricep extension lowers a bell behind the head from an upright seat, the back support removing any temptation to arch. Keep the elbows narrow and pointing forward and let the bell sink deep behind the head; the triceps work hardest in the bottom half.",
    "side_lying_lateral_raise": "The side-lying lateral raise raises a bell from a side-lying position, loading the deltoid of the bottom arm through its strictest arc. The floor removes all swing; raise to just past vertical and lower slowly, since the bottom half of the range is where this version earns its keep.",
    "single_arm_chest_supported_dumbbell_row": "The single-arm chest-supported dumbbell row rows one bell from a chest-down bench position, letting each side of the back work through its own range with zero momentum. Pull the elbow past the ribs and rotate the shoulder blade at the top; the pad keeps the spine honest.",
    "single_arm_dumbbell_overhead_tricep_extension": "The single-arm dumbbell overhead tricep extension lowers one bell behind the head, letting the elbow track its own natural path while the triceps work in isolation. Keep the upper arm vertical and close to the ear; support the working arm with the free hand at the elbow.",
    "single_arm_hammer_curl": "The single-arm hammer curl curls one bell with a neutral grip, letting each arm work without the stronger side hiding. Keep the elbow pinned to the ribs and squeeze the bell at the top; the wrist stays locked as if swinging a hammer.",
    "single_dumbbell_svend_press": "The single dumbbell Svend press squeezes the top of a bell between the palms and presses it out at chest height, an isometric pec squeeze layered onto a press. Keep the squeeze constant, since the press range can be short; the chest should tremble before the arms do.",
    "spider_curl": "The spider curl curls two bells from a chest-down incline position with the arms hanging vertical, isolating the biceps at their shortest position. With the shoulder fixed and the elbows unsupported, nothing can swing; squeeze hard at the top and lower for a three-count.",
    "strict_curl": "The strict curl curls a barbell with the back flat against a wall, removing every ounce of hip and shoulder drive. Keep the elbows pinned and the head, shoulders and glutes touching the wall; it is the purest test of biceps strength there is.",
    "zottman_curl": "The Zottman curl curls a bell with the palm up, then rotates at the top and lowers it with the palm down, training the biceps on the way up and the brachialis and extensors on the way down. Keep the rotation smooth at the top and the lowering slow; the eccentric is the point.",
}
